work_order.approach:
One file. No production code, no second file, no new dependency.

In `packages/desktop-client/e2e/command-bar.test.ts`, on top of what PR #60 already landed:

1. Replace the single `DIALOG_WARNING_PROBE` constant with `DIALOG_WARNING_PROBES`, an array holding one verbatim Radix message per alternative in `dialogAccessibilityWarning`, each tagged with the console channel Radix emits it on. The two strings are transcribed from `@radix-ui/react-dialog@1.1.15` and nothing else — do not retype them from the doc comment, copy them from the installed package (`node_modules/@radix-ui/react-dialog/dist/index.mjs`) and check them byte for byte:
   - title warning, emitted by Radix with `console.error`: `` `DialogContent` requires a `DialogTitle` for the component to be accessible for screen reader users. `` — that is the FIRST LINE of Radix's three-paragraph warning; the remaining two paragraphs are prose addressed to a developer, the filter matches inside the first line, and the probe emits the first line alone.
   - description warning, emitted by Radix with `console.warn`: `Warning: Missing `Description` or `aria-describedby={undefined}` for {DialogContent}.`
   Give the array an explicit element type rather than `as const` — Playwright's `page.evaluate` argument has to stay assignable to its serialisable parameter type, and a readonly tuple is a needless fight. The `error`/`warn` channel tags matter: the collector in `watchDialogWarnings` accepts only console types `error` and `warning`, and Radix puts these two messages on different ones. A probe that emitted both through `console.warn` would keep passing if someone later narrowed the collector to one channel.

2. Add a static coverage assertion at the top of the probe block, before anything is emitted, so the property that was missing is now enforced by the test rather than described by a comment:

   for (const alternative of dialogAccessibilityWarning.source.split('|')) {
     expect(
       DIALOG_WARNING_PROBES.some(({ message }) => new RegExp(alternative).test(message)),
       `no probe exercises the filter alternative: ${alternative}`,
     ).toBe(true);
   }

   This is what makes the fix general rather than a patch of this instance: add a third alternative to the filter later and this fails, naming the alternative, until a third probe exists. It costs nothing at runtime — no browser round-trip, no timing, no flake surface — and it fails with a better message than the poll would. Comment the naive `split('|')` honestly: it is correct for this flat alternation and would need revisiting if the pattern ever gained a group or an escaped `|`.

3. Replace the single-probe emission with one that emits both, each on its own channel, and poll for the whole set:

   const probe = watchDialogWarnings(page);
   await page.evaluate(probes => {
     for (const { channel, message } of probes) {
       if (channel === 'error') {
         console.error(message);
       } else {
         console.warn(message);
       }
     }
   }, DIALOG_WARNING_PROBES);
   await expect
     .poll(() => [...probe.messages].sort(), { timeout: CONSOLE_SETTLE_MS })
     .toEqual(DIALOG_WARNING_PROBES.map(({ message }) => message).sort());

   Both sides are sorted because CDP delivery order between two console events is not guaranteed and an order-sensitive assertion would be a flake. Equality rather than `includes` also proves the collector holds each message exactly once. The explicit `if` is deliberate — the repo's own `prefer-if-statement` rule wants it over a ternary. The block stays after `expect(messages).toEqual([])` for the reason already in the comment there: the probes match the same pattern and would poison the buffer they are meant to be independent of.

4. Rewrite the two comments — the constant's doc block and the inline block above the probe — so the claim they make is one the code now keeps. They may say that every alternative in the filter is exercised by its own probe on every run, that the coverage assertion fails if an alternative has none, and that the probe still does NOT prove Radix's current build emits these strings. Drop 'the filter still matches' as a singular claim. Leave the `dialogAccessibilityWarning` doc comment itself alone: it keeps the `@radix-ui/react-dialog@1.1.15` pin, keeps both verbatim messages, and keeps no line numbers (AC-5).

5. Do not touch `reports.test.ts`, the release note, or any other file. The release note already shipped in this PR and its one-line body describes the same unreleased change; editing it can only put AC-8's validated shape at risk.

6. Run one new control and record it in `implementer-reply.md` at the repo root, next to the two AC-6 controls, with a `git status` line confirming the tree is clean afterwards: break ONLY the title arm — change the pattern's first alternative from `DialogContent. requires a .DialogTitle` to `DialogContent requires a DialogTitle`, the exact form dbcdcee shipped — and run `E2E_START_URL=<preview> yarn workspace @actual-app/web run playwright test command-bar.test.ts --browser=chromium --retries=0 -g "dialog accessibility"`. The test must now FAIL; before this change it passed, and that passing run is the bug. It should fail at the coverage assertion naming `DialogContent requires a DialogTitle`; if it fails later at the `expect.poll` timeout instead, that is still correct — record which assertion actually fired. Do not touch the PR body: .github/agents/pr-and-commit-rules.md forbids filling in the template and the pipeline posts implementer-reply.md as the comment beginning `## The implementer's answer`. If the sandbox cannot start the dev server, say exactly that in implementer-reply.md and name what was attempted; do not report an outcome you did not observe.
